  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unseasonably mild through Friday with 2.5 weeks-\nlong dry spell now over.\n\nAnomalous upper level ridging and south-southwest flow aloft\nwill maintain unseasonably warm temperatures through late week.\nMax/Min temps will be 10 to 20 degrees above the historical \naverage for the first week of November, but should fall short of\ndaily record highs (see climate section). \n\nA leading frontal band of light rain moved through the area\novernight with some trailing scattered showers. Fcst rainfall \namounts are generally between a tenth and three tenths of an\ninch through 00Z Fri with some locally higher amounts possible.\nThis officially ends the 2.5 weeks- long dry spell, which ranked\nin the top 15 longest such streaks at most locations around\nCentral PA.\n\nThe front slows/stalls near the I95 corridor tonight. A dry slot may\nkeep most locations dry this afternoon into Friday following \nthe initial period of rain. A shortwave trough/closed H5 low \ndropping southeast from the Great Lakes is expected to \nreinvigorate precipitation along the nearly stationary frontal \nzone on Friday which could clip the far southeast zones/Lower \nSusquehanna Valley. Model trends have been shifting this rain \nfarther to the east/closer to the coast over the past few \ncycles. After approaching 70F on Friday afternoon in the\nsoutheast, temperatures will drop quickly Friday night as cooler\nair filters in behind the cold front. Lows by Saturday morning\nrange from the mid 30s west of I-99 to the mid 40s in the\nsoutheast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending cooler this weekend with lake enhanced \nrain showers beneath upper level low\n\nSeasonably cooler/breezy pattern to start the first full \nweekend of November. The passage of closed H5 low followed by \nmean cyclonic flow favors persistent clouds and periods of lake\nenhanced rain over the western and central Alleghenies on \nSaturday. We were keen to manually adjust max temps a few\ndegrees lower on 11/7 to account for the cooler pattern shift\nwhich looks to continue into early next week. If the current\nforecast holds, it will be the first below average daytime high\nin three weeks!",
